    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `label_ticket` in `app/labeler.py`, but **the requests are not sent with temperature 0.** The pinned `anthropic==1.8.0` removed `temperature` from `Messages.create()`. The installed signature (`.venv/.../anthropic/resources/messages/messages.py:942`) has no `temperature`, `top_k` or `top_p` parameters, and the project's CLAUDE.md says not to pass them.\n\n**What it does:**\n- Sends one request to `MODEL` with `max_tokens=5`.\n- The system prompt is `\"Answer with exactly one word from: bug, feature, question.\"`, and the ticket text is the user message.\n- It joins the text blocks of the reply, strips and lower-cases the result, and returns it if it's in `LABELS`. Otherwise it returns `\"question\"`.\n\n`pytest -q` passes, but the only test checks that the labels are lowercase. Nothing tests `label_ticket` itself, and I didn't make a real API call.\n\n**Why I didn't force temperature in:** I could send `extra_body={\"temperature\": 0}` to get past the SDK. I didn't, because the SDK removing the parameter suggests the API may no longer accept it either. In that case every labeling call could fail, and the project notes say not to pass it. Upgrading or downgrading the SDK isn't allowed here either.\n\n**Decision for you:** Without temperature, the labeler isn't guaranteed to be deterministic. The 5-token limit, the single-word prompt and the fallback to `\"question\"` keep the output to one of the three labels, but the same ticket could still get different labels on different runs. You can accept that, or I can add the `extra_body` workaround if you know the API still accepts `temperature` for this model."
  },
